WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.636
Hey, how's it going?

00:00:00.636 --> 00:00:03.898
Welcome back to&nbsp;another episode of TwoSet Violin!

00:00:03.898 --> 00:00:06.300
Today's video is a continuation...

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.800
...from&nbsp;our Pictionary video with Hilary Hahn.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.238
After that game, we actually ended up just chatting about

00:00:11.238 --> 00:00:15.122
a wide array of musical and violin related topics.

00:00:15.122 --> 00:00:16.472
I'm just making the intro for this,

00:00:16.472 --> 00:00:18.609
because&nbsp;we realised it'd be a bit weird to

00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:20.822
just kind of jump straight into it.

00:00:20.822 --> 00:00:21.846
It was actually quite&nbsp;an...

00:00:21.846 --> 00:00:22.499
I N T E R E S T I N G

00:00:22.499 --> 00:00:23.147
...conversation,

00:00:23.147 --> 00:00:24.746
we talked about new violins,

00:00:24.746 --> 00:00:29.034
we talked about AI and how it might&nbsp;interact with music,

00:00:29.034 --> 00:00:30.663
and we thought, "You know what?"

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.011
"Let's share this."

00:00:32.011 --> 00:00:34.244
I definitely found the&nbsp;conversation very insightful,

00:00:34.244 --> 00:00:35.000
and so, yeah.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.497
We were planning to make an official video

00:00:37.497 --> 00:00:39.489
announcing our new violins later,

00:00:39.489 --> 00:00:42.546
because Brett just got his about one to two weeks ago,

00:00:42.546 --> 00:00:45.552
and&nbsp;we wanted some time to play the violins in a bit,

00:00:45.552 --> 00:00:48.002
because sometimes, a brand new violin,

00:00:48.002 --> 00:00:50.624
it takes&nbsp;time before it can speak,

00:00:50.624 --> 00:00:53.143
and before you can get to know it more intimately.

00:00:53.143 --> 00:00:55.443
But of course, in this&nbsp;video with Hilary,

00:00:55.443 --> 00:00:56.702
we talked about our new violins,

00:00:56.702 --> 00:00:58.465
so I thought it wouldn't really make sense

00:00:58.465 --> 00:00:59.854
unless we told you guys,

00:00:59.854 --> 00:01:02.241
so here's the unofficial announcement.

00:01:02.241 --> 00:01:04.035
[Unfortunately] Brett's not here with&nbsp;me right now,

00:01:04.035 --> 00:01:05.269
he's actually resting,

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.954
because he's feeling a little bit under the weather.

00:01:07.954 --> 00:01:10.652
I think&nbsp;we'll be doing an official video,

00:01:10.652 --> 00:01:13.600
but for now I guess I can just show you guys my new violin.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.181
It's Kurt Widenhouse, he's an American maker,

00:01:16.181 --> 00:01:19.050
and uh, he was recommended to me from Ray, actually.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.808
So&nbsp;thank you, Ray, for the recommendation,

00:01:20.808 --> 00:01:21.960
I really like this violin.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.000
Um, I've been needing a new violin&nbsp;for the longest time,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.990
because I've been playing before then,

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:31.478
on the same violin that I had&nbsp;been playing on since I was...

00:01:31.478 --> 00:01:33.181
...I think nine or ten years old.

00:01:33.181 --> 00:01:35.037
I was long overdue for a new violin.

00:01:35.037 --> 00:01:36.996
But yeah, it's beautiful, I love it.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.348
Um...

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.446
It's amazing, because it's still being played&nbsp;in.

00:01:50.446 --> 00:01:53.848
Every day, when I practice on it, it's still opening up more,

00:01:53.848 --> 00:01:55.669
so I'm really excited&nbsp;to see kind of...

00:01:55.669 --> 00:01:58.120
...where the sound develops for this violin.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:01:59.844
Look, the varnish is so nice,

00:01:59.844 --> 00:02:00.833
look at that varnish.

00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:02.764
I think, on another day,

00:02:02.764 --> 00:02:05.108
when Brett's violin's more&nbsp;played in as well,

00:02:05.475 --> 00:02:07.246
we might do another video to talk about it.

00:02:07.246 --> 00:02:09.337
But for now, let's shoot right&nbsp;into this discussion

00:02:09.337 --> 00:02:10.718
that we had with Hilary.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.609
It's...it's a weird year.

00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:15.927
I mean, I had a lot of&nbsp;stuff lined up that was really cool,

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:17.280
but I'm also...

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.515
Since that stuff has sort of been cancelled,

00:02:19.515 --> 00:02:20.678
some&nbsp;of it, um...

00:02:20.678 --> 00:02:24.705
I've been looking into other ways to...

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.850
...create, and collaborate, and build projects,

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.774
and so,&nbsp;I've got some stuff coming up,

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:33.183
like a intersection of art and AI,

00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:35.904
collaborated with some people for&nbsp;a dance project,

00:02:35.904 --> 00:02:37.802
some recording stuff coming out,

00:02:37.802 --> 00:02:40.000
so there's a lot coming up,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.996
and I'm really excited to&nbsp;share it,

00:02:41.996 --> 00:02:44.467
and it's gonna be sort of rolling out every month,

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:45.731
for the rest of the year.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.908
So we'll&nbsp;just kind of have to all stay in touch,

00:02:48.908 --> 00:02:50.560
and stay tuned.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.570
Yeah, wow, that sounds super exciting.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.628
But yeah, it'll be on my...on my social media,

00:02:55.628 --> 00:02:59.880
I'm pretty active on Instagram and Twitter, but&nbsp;also YouTube,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.700
we're trying to update the channel with some more performances,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.133
so as soon as&nbsp;YouTube stops taking me down,

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:07.443
we can get my recordings to stay up,

00:03:07.443 --> 00:03:08.960
- there will be more&nbsp;to watch, too.
- What?!

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.916
YouTube's taking you down?!

00:03:10.916 --> 00:03:11.721
Yeah!

00:03:11.721 --> 00:03:12.518
Oh...!

00:03:12.518 --> 00:03:14.643
Yeah, every time I post a video,

00:03:14.643 --> 00:03:18.446
- it gets flagged as...infringement,
- Flagged...oh, yeah...

00:03:18.446 --> 00:03:20.646
- and so, we're trying to track down...
- You...oh...

00:03:20.646 --> 00:03:23.966
- That is so annoying.
- ...all of the, like all of the things,

00:03:23.966 --> 00:03:25.539
'cause you enter a&nbsp;complaint,

00:03:25.539 --> 00:03:27.843
and it takes 30 days to get resolved, and you might post it,

00:03:27.843 --> 00:03:29.471
and it gets taken down again.

00:03:29.584 --> 00:03:32.782
So I've got a bunch of material that I'm ready to post,

00:03:32.782 --> 00:03:35.917
so as soon as we can get that sorted&nbsp;out, YouTube.

00:03:36.031 --> 00:03:37.669
- Oh...
- Yes.
- We'll have a lot more to look at.

00:03:37.669 --> 00:03:39.328
Alright, YouTube, did you hear that?

00:03:39.328 --> 00:03:41.798
This is Hilary Hahn's&nbsp;music, it's her playing.

00:03:41.798 --> 00:03:43.520
Officially complaining here!

00:03:44.389 --> 00:03:47.307
It's the OG music, everyone needs to hear.

00:03:47.307 --> 00:03:49.438
Yeah,&nbsp;everyone should just enjoy music right now,

00:03:49.438 --> 00:03:52.146
I think it's so connective, and so beautiful,

00:03:52.146 --> 00:03:55.585
and&nbsp;it's expressive even for the listeners,

00:03:55.585 --> 00:03:58.289
so if you just find something you love listening to,

00:03:58.289 --> 00:04:01.107
just keep listening to it and really enjoy,

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:05.132
and just hang out with the music community as much&nbsp;as you can online,

00:04:05.132 --> 00:04:06.328
that will get us through, I think.

00:04:06.328 --> 00:04:07.748
It's hard to be like,

00:04:07.748 --> 00:04:09.600
"Yeah! Everything's awesome!"

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.222
- But,&nbsp;there are awesome things in the world right now,
- Yeah.

00:04:13.222 --> 00:04:14.623
that are between people,

00:04:14.623 --> 00:04:16.945
and I think that's the&nbsp;essence of art, right?

00:04:16.945 --> 00:04:21.304
It's like art continues through whatever is happening in the world,

00:04:21.304 --> 00:04:25.120
and it's a place for people to be, and feel, and...

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.718
...connect, and share the humanity of their&nbsp;experiences in the world.

00:04:30.718 --> 00:04:33.355
So this time is really hard,

00:04:33.355 --> 00:04:35.810
and it's also got so many

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.560
really&nbsp;beautiful moments of connection in it,

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.551
and it's interesting to be an artist in&nbsp;this time,

00:04:41.551 --> 00:04:44.291
and it's also interesting to be making things

00:04:44.291 --> 00:04:45.920
that connect with people.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.146
It's a very layered...thought process,

00:04:49.146 --> 00:04:51.840
as uh...as anyone working in music right now.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.600
Yeah, definitely.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.119
I mean, I couldn't imagine going&nbsp;through

00:04:56.119 --> 00:04:58.356
2020 without music, right?

00:04:58.356 --> 00:05:00.118
That would just be...so depressing.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.824
Yeah, I had two weeks where I was in the same room

00:05:02.824 --> 00:05:04.981
as live music, in all of 2020,

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.852
because I had my sabbatical, and then stuff&nbsp;got cancelled,

00:05:08.852 --> 00:05:10.285
or I couldn't get there,

00:05:10.285 --> 00:05:12.420
and then I had two weeks, and it was amazing.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.154
I played in&nbsp;Dallas and in Houston,

00:05:14.154 --> 00:05:17.267
it was great to be back on stage, it was so vibrant,

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:20.944
we were all wearing masks,&nbsp;and everyone was on a single stand,

00:05:20.944 --> 00:05:23.403
like...no one had a stand partner,

00:05:23.403 --> 00:05:25.121
everyone was turning their&nbsp;own pages,

00:05:25.121 --> 00:05:26.538
and there was space between everyone,

00:05:26.538 --> 00:05:31.262
- but yet it felt so...familiar, it felt so vibrant, and so real.
- Yeah.

00:05:31.262 --> 00:05:33.260
Wait, did you say everyone had masks on?

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:34.813
Yeah, yeah, everyone was masked.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.578
What about&nbsp;the wind players?

00:05:36.578 --> 00:05:37.673
Except for the wind players.

00:05:37.673 --> 00:05:40.240
They...they have to space...

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.701
I've seen different performances,

00:05:41.701 --> 00:05:44.531
like some&nbsp;wind players playing under shields.

00:05:44.531 --> 00:05:45.999
- Brett and Eddy: Ooh!
- Hilary: I've seen that.

00:05:45.999 --> 00:05:46.768
That's cool.

00:05:46.768 --> 00:05:49.727
In these cases, there was&nbsp;just a lot of space,

00:05:49.727 --> 00:05:52.503
so everyone around me had a mask on.

00:05:52.503 --> 00:05:55.151
It's a new thing, to play with&nbsp;the mask,

00:05:55.151 --> 00:05:57.588
and it changes your sight lines,

00:05:57.588 --> 00:05:58.560
- and...
- Yeah!

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.500
For pianists, like they...

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.056
- Their peripheral vision,&nbsp;towards their hands,
- Oh, yeah!

00:06:03.056 --> 00:06:05.000
the mask...gets in the way.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.513
And I found also with the mask on playing,

00:06:07.513 --> 00:06:09.900
it&nbsp;changed my perception of my sound,

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.575
because I used to play with a cloth on the chin rest,

00:06:12.575 --> 00:06:14.060
and&nbsp;so that was familiar,

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.640
having something between my chin and the chin rest.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.481
But the mask, next to&nbsp;my face,

00:06:19.481 --> 00:06:23.040
changed how the sound traveled to my ear,

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.222
- from the violin.
- Yeah.

00:06:24.222 --> 00:06:29.057
I didn't even realise that my&nbsp;face was a sound transmitter,

00:06:29.057 --> 00:06:33.986
but with a sort of acoustic absorber on my cheek, I guess,

00:06:33.986 --> 00:06:36.315
I don't&nbsp;know, like somehow it changed the sound.

00:06:36.315 --> 00:06:38.717
- It was so bizarre.
- Yeah, that would be so weird.

00:06:38.717 --> 00:06:40.814
Very subtle, though, very subtle.

00:06:40.814 --> 00:06:42.951
But like, it's easy to force then,

00:06:42.951 --> 00:06:44.809
so you have to just play by feel,

00:06:44.809 --> 00:06:46.236
otherwise you would&nbsp;push too hard,

00:06:46.236 --> 00:06:48.064
'cause you can't hear it as well,

00:06:48.064 --> 00:06:49.822
you're like "I gotta play louder!"

00:06:49.822 --> 00:06:52.743
I mean, it's definitely&nbsp;better to have sound travel

00:06:52.743 --> 00:06:54.880
than to have corona travel, right?

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.051
Oh, no, I'm not complaining about&nbsp;wearing a mask at all,

00:06:58.051 --> 00:06:59.823
it enabled us to perform,

00:06:59.823 --> 00:07:02.080
and if that's all it takes, I'm in.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:04.852
It was just&nbsp;really nice to be able to do that,

00:07:04.852 --> 00:07:07.150
and know that everyone was safe at the same time,

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:08.372
including the&nbsp;audience.

00:07:08.372 --> 00:07:12.080
And now we go into a time when things...

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.424
...in my calendar have changed a bit for the next few&nbsp;months,

00:07:15.424 --> 00:07:17.398
but it's like, it's there.

00:07:17.398 --> 00:07:20.639
It's waiting, and everything's just gonna spring back into action

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:22.289
when it's...when it's time.

00:07:22.289 --> 00:07:23.878
Hopefully for us as well, um,

00:07:23.878 --> 00:07:25.440
I miss going on tour,

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.648
- it's been so long since&nbsp;we've had our last proper tour.
- I know.

00:07:29.648 --> 00:07:31.718
And we should try our violins as well, Eddy.

00:07:31.718 --> 00:07:33.269
In concert&nbsp;halls, we've never done that.

00:07:33.269 --> 00:07:35.000
Oh yeah, we got new violins!

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.480
You did? Where are they?

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:37.486
They're here with&nbsp;us.

00:07:37.486 --> 00:07:39.840
But Eddy got a Widenhouse...the maker.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:40.838
Where is he based out of?

00:07:40.838 --> 00:07:42.593
It's an American violin maker.

00:07:42.593 --> 00:07:44.031
Wait Eddy, where is he based again?

00:07:44.031 --> 00:07:45.760
North Carolina?

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.789
Yeah, Ray recommended him to us.

00:07:47.789 --> 00:07:50.462
Modern violin, but for the value,

00:07:50.462 --> 00:07:52.071
the sound it makes is...

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.648
I'm quite happy with it.

00:07:53.648 --> 00:07:56.378
It's about the match, it's not about the maker.

00:07:56.378 --> 00:07:58.518
i think it's about the match with [the] performer.

00:07:58.518 --> 00:07:59.754
That's true, that's true.

00:07:59.754 --> 00:08:00.863
- 100 percent.
- Yeah.

00:08:00.863 --> 00:08:02.992
For me, it wasn't also just the match thing, it was [that]

00:08:02.992 --> 00:08:07.262
I was so in desperate need of an upgrade of&nbsp;a violin, so...

00:08:07.262 --> 00:08:11.680
'Cause my old violin just wouldn't project anymore, in the halls.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:13.411
It's a different&nbsp;set of needs.

00:08:13.411 --> 00:08:15.961
When you have a violin that needs to project,

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:20.000
versus a violin that you can play in&nbsp;an ensemble,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.744
it's...it's just a totally different beast.

00:08:22.744 --> 00:08:27.044
And you need something with more power or&nbsp;more...polish,

00:08:27.044 --> 00:08:28.320
or something.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:31.474
Not literally polish, like finesse, maybe.

00:08:31.474 --> 00:08:33.238
If someone watching&nbsp;this just starts

00:08:33.238 --> 00:08:36.800
- getting out the nail polish and polishes the violin...
- Someone's like, "I just need more polish on my violin!"

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.061
Maybe you can do that for Wieniawski,

00:08:39.061 --> 00:08:40.620
'cause he's Polish, get it?

00:08:40.742 --> 00:08:43.301
- *groans*
- That's a horrible joke.

00:08:43.743 --> 00:08:44.885
Dad joke.

00:08:44.885 --> 00:08:45.620
So bad.

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:46.419
I'm sorry.

00:08:46.419 --> 00:08:48.405
That's what&nbsp;Brett has to put up with every day.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.078
It's alright, he composes "Poland-naise".

00:08:52.078 --> 00:08:53.164
Poland?

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.313
No,&nbsp;that's not funny.

00:08:56.493 --> 00:08:57.439
Alright, that's enough.

00:08:57.439 --> 00:08:59.469
Is Brett also playing a new instrument?

00:08:59.469 --> 00:09:00.351
Oh yeah.

00:09:00.351 --> 00:09:00.978
So Eddy&nbsp;got his,

00:09:00.978 --> 00:09:02.240
I was like, "Oh, I want one too."

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.842
So I just got mine.

00:09:03.842 --> 00:09:05.000
Is it the same maker?

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.298
Same maker as well.

00:09:06.298 --> 00:09:08.366
Um, I just got&nbsp;it, so I'm still deciding.

00:09:08.366 --> 00:09:09.178
First initial thoughts,

00:09:09.178 --> 00:09:11.832
- it's already much better than my previous violin. 
- Mm-hmm.

00:09:11.832 --> 00:09:14.607
But compared to Eddy's, I think it's a little bit more closed.

00:09:14.607 --> 00:09:16.866
But it's a lot sweeter, so I'm hoping...

00:09:16.866 --> 00:09:18.883
I'm playing it, I don't know how long it normally takes,

00:09:18.883 --> 00:09:20.406
for it to kind of start opening up.

00:09:20.406 --> 00:09:22.620
Do you know&nbsp;how to play in a violin?

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.000
Oh, there's a way?

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.040
- Yeah, I did not know that, I was...
- Yeah, my way!
- Wait what?

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:28.160
- I have a way.
- Oh!

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:31.742
So sometimes I'll go to violin&nbsp;shops and just play instruments

00:09:31.742 --> 00:09:33.682
that haven't been played a lot,

00:09:33.682 --> 00:09:36.068
just as like a favor to a friend&nbsp;or whatever,

00:09:36.068 --> 00:09:38.080
if they have a new violin that they just made,

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.539
or if it's a shop where I'm just trying&nbsp;instruments for fun,

00:09:42.539 --> 00:09:45.995
I'll like, pay it forward by then playing on something

00:09:45.995 --> 00:09:49.572
that hasn't had enough&nbsp;hours to really show itself yet.

00:09:49.572 --> 00:09:51.154
The way I do it is I take—

00:09:51.154 --> 00:09:52.660
I give it like an hour,

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:57.333
and just in the&nbsp;course of the hour, I play as loud as possible,

00:09:57.333 --> 00:10:01.114
- Oh, right.
- for about a minute or so at a time,

00:10:01.114 --> 00:10:03.092
and then I play&nbsp;close to the bridge,

00:10:03.092 --> 00:10:07.533
and I really try to make it like ugly, edgy, loud.

00:10:07.533 --> 00:10:11.342
I just find the edge&nbsp;of the sound, and then I play really quietly,

00:10:11.342 --> 00:10:13.987
and I find the edge of where the tone is.

00:10:13.987 --> 00:10:17.200
It's&nbsp;like a rubber band, like you keep stretching it,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.729
and letting it relax.

00:10:18.729 --> 00:10:20.348
Stretch it, let it relax.

00:10:20.348 --> 00:10:22.804
And&nbsp;then whatever it's not comfortable doing,

00:10:22.804 --> 00:10:28.222
you just do that magnified, for like five, ten&nbsp;minutes.

00:10:28.222 --> 00:10:30.400
So like if it doesn't wanna...

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:31.962
...speak with harmonics,

00:10:31.962 --> 00:10:35.359
it could be that the wood is&nbsp;like, tight in that area,

00:10:35.359 --> 00:10:37.120
or maybe it could be that...

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:39.594
...you're not comfortable with the way

00:10:39.594 --> 00:10:41.277
you need&nbsp;to draw out the sound,

00:10:41.277 --> 00:10:43.121
or maybe the resonance patterns

00:10:43.121 --> 00:10:44.699
haven't established themselves yet,

00:10:44.699 --> 00:10:47.718
so you have a chance to really shape the new instruments,

00:10:47.718 --> 00:10:49.440
and shape how they vibrate.

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:51.887
That's&nbsp;why when I...when I'm playing a new instrument,

00:10:51.887 --> 00:10:52.890
and I'm thinking,

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:55.226
"There's something that's&nbsp;not happening right here,"

00:10:55.226 --> 00:10:59.853
I really try to dig...into it, and find that, and draw it out.

00:10:59.853 --> 00:11:02.853
And&nbsp;remarkably it actually does happen,

00:11:02.853 --> 00:11:05.143
like in the course of an hour, a violin...

00:11:05.143 --> 00:11:08.108
A new violin&nbsp;will change significantly with

00:11:08.108 --> 00:11:10.883
being pushed to its limits.

00:11:10.883 --> 00:11:12.840
And notes-wise, you just play any note?

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:13.760
On all the strings?

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:15.397
Yeah, like you can just...

00:11:15.691 --> 00:11:17.461
I mean, you can play a piece,

00:11:17.461 --> 00:11:19.675
but, it's good to stay,

00:11:19.675 --> 00:11:24.457
like to&nbsp;exercise the violin evenly on all the strings, so...

00:11:24.457 --> 00:11:26.001
...you can kind of play the same thing,

00:11:26.001 --> 00:11:28.805
but transcribe&nbsp;it up a fifth, and transcribe it up a fifth,

00:11:28.805 --> 00:11:30.160
- and transcribe&nbsp;it up a fifth.
- Yeah, okay.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:32.083
So that you use the whole violin,

00:11:32.083 --> 00:11:34.223
But you&nbsp;can just do like long slow bows,

00:11:34.223 --> 00:11:35.338
or fast, like...

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.528
And you can play repertoire,

00:11:38.528 --> 00:11:41.270
you can do like the&nbsp;opening of the Brahms concerto,

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:42.919
but you don't go quiet.

00:11:42.919 --> 00:11:44.829
It's like a full workout, for the violin.

00:11:44.829 --> 00:11:47.124
Yeah,&nbsp;or you can play something that's normally quiet

00:11:47.124 --> 00:11:48.960
but it's the right range.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.624
But I do like to spend a&nbsp;good 10 minutes

00:11:50.624 --> 00:11:53.243
playing it as quietly as possible,

00:11:53.243 --> 00:11:55.200
sort of across the instrument.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.139
In the end of that,

00:11:56.139 --> 00:12:00.291
- you kind of have a picture of what the violin is capable of,
- Mmm.
- Okay.

00:12:00.291 --> 00:12:04.270
and then you can see, maybe it's an&nbsp;adjustment thing,

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:07.148
if you have a chance to get an adjustment for the violin,

00:12:07.148 --> 00:12:10.424
that can help, but you&nbsp;don't want to go in and get it adjusted

00:12:10.424 --> 00:12:13.596
if you haven't really exercised it and drawn it out.

00:12:13.596 --> 00:12:16.134
Play it out, play it out for a couple of weeks,

00:12:16.702 --> 00:12:20.511
and then you see if it's settling into patterns.

00:12:20.511 --> 00:12:22.427
Okay, I know what I'm doing for the next two weeks.

00:12:22.427 --> 00:12:24.480
I just got it, a few days ago, so...

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:26.954
- Well Eddy, you're gonna hear a lot of crunchy noises,
- Good luck.

00:12:26.954 --> 00:12:28.254
for the next two weeks.

00:12:28.254 --> 00:12:29.639
It's interesting you said&nbsp;that,

00:12:29.639 --> 00:12:32.080
'cause I think I have been subconsciously...

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:32.790
...doing that,

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:35.000
not so much belt it as loud&nbsp;as possible,

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:38.248
or playing close to the bridge, but I did notice...

00:12:38.248 --> 00:12:41.155
Sometimes when I would play&nbsp;really loud passages

00:12:41.155 --> 00:12:43.067
by the end of a practice session,

00:12:43.067 --> 00:12:46.007
the violin would be more resonant, or&nbsp;could take more.

